<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Teungku Chik di Tiro</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1836">1836</a>–<a href="/years/1891">1891</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Muhammad Saman</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Teungku Chik di Tiro, born Muhammad Saman, was an Acehnese religious scholar who led resistance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh-war">Aceh War</a> from the early 1880s. Proclaiming the struggle a holy war, he rallied village fighters and recovered much of the countryside, confining the Dutch to fortified lines around the capital. He died in 1891, reportedly poisoned, and is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-hero-of-indonesia">National Hero of Indonesia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/teungku-chik-di-tiro.jpg" alt="Teungku Chik di Tiro" width="320" height="342" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Amrullah tintaemasnet · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tgk._Chik_di_Tiro.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>He was born in 1836 in Tiro, in Pedir in the Aceh Sultanate, to Tengku Syekh Abdullah and Siti Aisyah. He studied with his father until he was 15, then with his uncle Teungku Chik Dayah Cut di Tiro and several other teachers, and spent two years in Aceh Besar, studying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> by day and joining friends in the fight against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> by night. Called home to teach with his uncle, he later went on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">hajj</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, where talks with Islamic leaders and revolutionaries from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a> deepened his resolve to fight the Dutch.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1880 a band of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> passing through Tiro sought an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">ulama</a> to lead them, and di Tiro joined them at their base on Mount Miram. He travelled across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> preaching holy war in the mosques and wrote to other ulama, determined to drive the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> out by 1883. With some 6,000 fighters and the support of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Aceh, his forces cut communications between Dutch forts and set up a base at Mureu.</p><p>In May 1881 they took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> fortress at Indrapuri, then those at Krueng Jreu and Gle Kameng, and the Dutch reinforced Lambaro, Aneuk Galong and Samahani. In early 1883 his men attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banda-aceh">Kutaraja</a>, now Banda Aceh, failing to take it but killing the Dutch controller; at the height of the war the Dutch held only 4 square kilometres.</p><p>In 1884, when the sultan was told that di Tiro had become the people's leader, the sultan declared that he was still sultan, and in August di Tiro stated that he had no wish to take the throne. In 1885 he offered the Assistant Resident Van Langen peace if the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and he wrote again in 1888 without reply.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 21 January 1891 he was served poisoned food by the son of the leader of Sagi, to whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> had offered a leadership post for killing him, and he died soon after being taken to the Aneuk Galong fortress for treatment. He was buried in the family graveyard at Meureu in Aceh Besar. Teuku Umar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cut-nyak-dhien">Cut Nyak Dhien</a>, Cut Nyak Meutia and his own family carried on the struggle. President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a> declared him a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-hero-of-indonesia">National Hero of Indonesia</a> on 6 November 1973, and streets bear his name, among them one in Menteng in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a>. He was the great-grandfather of Hasan di Tiro, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-aceh-movement">Free Aceh Movement</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Teungku Chik di Tiro” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Aceh War against the Netherlands from 1873, Acehnese fighters defended fortified villages and later waged a long guerrilla war under leaders such as <b>Teungku Chik di Tiro</b>, Teuku Umar and Cut Nyak Dhien, inflicting heavy losses on the Dutch colonial army.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aceh-sultanate">Aceh Sultanate · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banda-aceh">Banda Aceh</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=5.57,95.34&amp;year=1871" aria-label="Aceh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Teungku Chik di Tiro"><image href="/maps/globes/g09000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5454 4524h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5454 4524h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=5.57,95.34&amp;year=1871">What was here in 1871? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
